<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  An emergency department where the room and the physician are different resources (Pro).

  Patients arrive about every 15 minutes (a Poisson stream), are triaged by one nurse (about 5 minutes), are treated by a physician in a room (about 20 minutes), and then
  recover in the SAME room (about 30 minutes) before leaving. There are 5 rooms and 2 physicians.
    resources="Room:5, Physician:2"      two pools of units
    needs="Treatment: Room + Physician"  a treatment starts only when a room AND a physician are free, and holds one of each
    keeps="Treatment: Room"              the room is not given back when the treatment ends: the patient keeps it for the recovery
    releases="Recovery: Room"            the room is given back when the recovery ends
  The physician goes back to the pool when the treatment ends, so the next patient waiting for a physician starts at once. Without keeps, the room would be free
  during recovery and the model would understate how often every room is full. The old way to write this (one station with min(rooms, physicians) servers) cannot show
  either shortage separately, and makes one of the two levers do nothing.
  Time is in minutes. Output columns, 9 per row: 0 time, 1 patient, 2 station (0 Triage, 1 Treatment, 2 Recovery), 3 event type (0 arrive, 1 start, 2 finish, 3 exit), 4 queue,
  5 busy, 6 patients in the department, then the free units after the event: 7 free rooms, 8 free physicians.
  The waiting time of a patient for treatment is the time from their arrive row to their start row at station 1; it includes waiting for a room or for a physician.

  To run it:        grind with a count of 20000 on samples/pro/process-flow-ed-resources.xml
  What to look for: over 20,000 patients (seed 7): 34 percent start treatment at once and the mean wait for treatment is 32 minutes. With the declare rooms set to 7: 44 percent and
                    19 minutes; with physicians set to 3: 61 percent and 10.5 minutes; with rooms set to 4: 16 percent and 96 minutes. Both pools are 67 percent busy on average, yet the
                    waits differ: the pools hold up each other.
-->
<dataset columns="time, patient, station, type, queue, busy, inSystem, freeRooms, freePhysicians" seed="20261015">
  <definitions>
    <declare name="rooms" value="5"/>
    <declare name="physicians" value="2"/>
  </definitions>
  <root node="ED"/>
  <nodes>
    <Exponential name="Gap" lambda="0.0666667"/>
    <Exponential name="TriageTime" lambda="0.2"/>
    <Exponential name="TreatTime" lambda="0.05"/>
    <Exponential name="RecoverTime" lambda="0.0333333"/>
    <ProcessFlow name="ED" stations="Triage:1, Treatment:inf, Recovery:inf" entry="Triage" routing="Triage>Treatment:1; Treatment>Recovery:1"
                 resources="Room:${rooms}, Physician:${physicians}" needs="Treatment: Room + Physician" keeps="Treatment: Room" releases="Recovery: Room">
      <input name="Gap" role="arrival"/>
      <input name="TriageTime" role="service.Triage"/>
      <input name="TreatTime" role="service.Treatment"/>
      <input name="RecoverTime" role="service.Recovery"/>
    </ProcessFlow>
  </nodes>
</dataset>
